<!--
  ===================================================================================================
  STRUCTURED LOGGING FOR THE CARDDEMO JAVA MODULE

  WHAT THIS FILE REPLACES. The migrated estate had no logging framework, no metrics and no tracing.
  Its ONLY diagnostic channel was the console display statement, of which the migration plan records
  217 across the 28 COBOL programs. Everything an operator could once read off a job's output must
  now be visible through this configuration, structured and correlated.

  PROVENANCE. Migrated from the CardDemo mainframe estate at commit
  7756d895ffeb65f7ea72aaa609e356d9899afcec, upstream release stamp CardDemo_v1.0-15-g27d6c6f-68
  dated 2022-07-19. The legacy estate is cited, never transcribed: no COBOL, job control, screen
  definition, copybook or CICS resource text appears in this module.

  DIVISION OF RESPONSIBILITY, AND IT IS A CROSS FILE CONTRACT. This file owns appender selection,
  the encoders, and the correlation fields. It owns NO verbosity policy: every level is owned by
  application.yml and its three overlays (application-local.yml, application-test.yml,
  application-prod.yml, plus the suite's own copy of the test overlay under src/test/resources), and
  all four of those files say so in their own comments. The framework applies logging.level entries
  AFTER this file is read, and a profile document MERGES with the shared baseline rather than
  replacing it, so for any category those files name, their value is the authority and the value
  stated here is only an agreeing baseline that keeps this file readable on its own. That is also
  why the four application categories below are declared INHERITED rather than pinned: the local
  overlay raises the whole com.carddemo tree to DEBUG, and pinning a descendant here would silently
  defeat it.

  THE FILE NAME IS LOAD BEARING. Named logback-spring.xml, not logback.xml, because the springProfile
  and springProperty elements exist only when the framework loads the configuration after preparing
  its environment. Under plain logback.xml the initialisation happens first and every springProfile
  block below would be ignored without a word of complaint, leaving one appender layout for every
  profile. Per profile appenders are a requirement, so the suffix is not a stylistic choice. The
  module's .gitignore and .dockerignore each carry an explicit negation for this exact path so the
  file is neither untracked nor left out of the container image.

  CORRELATION IDENTIFIERS ARE AN ALLOW LIST, NOT THE WHOLE DIAGNOSTIC CONTEXT. The tracing bridge
  publishes the current trace and span into the logging context under the keys traceId and spanId, which
  are what joins a log line to its exported trace. The JSON encoder emits exactly those two keys. The
  logging context is a thread-local map any code on the request thread can write, including a library
  this module did not author, and the machine-facing appender is the one whose output leaves the process;
  exporting the whole map would publish every future key with no review at the point it is added, and the
  values this application handles include a card primary account number, a card verification code, a
  government-issued identifier and a sign-on credential. An allow list makes a new key invisible until
  someone adds it here deliberately. The readable pattern spells the same two keys out explicitly rather
  than relying on the optional correlation segment of the framework's own console pattern, so the
  identifiers are present whether or not that segment has been populated.

  THE CONSTRAINT ON WHAT THIS CONFIGURATION EMITS. No key is added to the allow list above without first
  establishing that it cannot carry a credential, a signing value, a bearer token, a full card primary
  account number or a national identifier. The two categories that would otherwise carry such a value out
  of the persistence provider - its statement text and its bound parameter values - are held below at a
  level that emits neither. What a given application message contains is decided by the code composing
  it, not by this file.

  DIAGNOSTIC ORDERING IS PRESERVED ON PURPOSE. On a batch terminal path the legacy programs wrote the
  error, then the raw two byte file status, and only then abended. The migrated batch tier keeps that
  order and keeps the raw status in the message, so an operator reading a Java log sees the same
  sequence, with the same code, that they read on the mainframe.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, each an explicit decision rather than an omission
    * No rolling file appender. The artefact runs as a container: its image writes the log to
      standard output and its health check reads the actuator health endpoint, so a file appender
      would write into an ephemeral layer that nothing collects.
    * No asynchronous appender and therefore no queue size. Every full-queue policy either blocks or
      discards events, and discarding would lose exactly the diagnostics an end-to-end output
      comparison is read to explain, so events are written on the calling thread.
    * No heap figure, no thread count, no latency, throughput, volume or availability number. No
      numeric service level is documented anywhere in the legacy estate, so performance for this
      implementation is measured and recorded rather than asserted.
    * No second logging backend, no additional bridge and no additional appender library. The only
      logging dependency beyond the framework's own logging starter is the structured encoder
      declared in pom.xml, and no version is restated here: the build owns versions.
    * No status listener that would swallow this file's own parse diagnostics. A configuration error
      must be loud.
  ===================================================================================================
-->
<configuration scan="false">

  <!-- The framework's own defaults, imported rather than restated: the colour, whitespace throwable
       and enclosed in brackets conversion words, the date, level and exception pattern properties
       reused below, the console charset, and the handful of container and validator categories the
       framework quietens by default. Nothing it already provides is redeclared here. -->
  <include resource="org/springframework/boot/logging/logback/defaults.xml"/>

  <!-- The service label, read from the one place that owns it. spring.application.name is set in
       application.yml and is the same label the meter registry carries as its `application` tag, so a
       log line and a metric can be filtered by one value. The fallback is deliberately NOT the real
       name: repeating it here would create a second place where the service is named, and a missing
       property should be visible in the output rather than quietly papered over.

       The active profile is deliberately not a field. It is reliably readable as a property only when
       the platform sets it, so a profile activated programmatically would produce a field that
       disagrees with reality, and the framework already states the active profiles on its own
       start-up line. -->
  <springProperty scope="context" name="applicationName" source="spring.application.name"
                  defaultValue="unknown"/>

  <!-- Correlation fields for the readable pattern, spelled out rather than delegated, so the trace
       and span identifiers appear in developer output exactly as they appear in the JSON. An event
       raised outside any request or job carries the empty default and stays aligned. -->
  <property name="CORRELATION_FIELDS" value="[${applicationName},%X{traceId:-},%X{spanId:-}]"/>

  <!-- The two bounds the machine readable throwable rendering is held to, declared here rather than
       inline so that both figures are read in one place and neither can be widened without the other
       being seen. The reasoning for bounding at all is on the appender that applies them.

       Frames per throwable. Chosen so that the frames that identify a failing migrated unit - the
       boundary handler, the service method standing in for the COBOL paragraph, and the collaborator
       beneath it - are inside the cap on every layer of the chain, while the repetitive framework and
       container tail below them is not. The cause chain itself is NOT capped: every cause is still
       named and still rendered, each to this depth, so a chain stays diagnosable at its full length.

       And the whole rendered trace. This is the backstop that makes one event's size independent of
       how deep a chain happens to be, and it is what removes log amplification as a lever: a caller
       who can provoke a failure carrying text they control cannot choose how many bytes it writes.
       Generous enough that a genuine multi-layer failure renders complete and never reaches it. -->
  <property name="JSON_THROWABLE_MAX_DEPTH_PER_THROWABLE" value="12"/>
  <property name="JSON_THROWABLE_MAX_LENGTH" value="8192"/>

  <!-- Human readable single line layout: instant, level, process, correlation, thread, category,
       message, and the throwable through the framework's exception conversion word. The date, level
       and exception segments reuse the imported properties so this pattern tracks the framework's
       own defaults instead of drifting from them. -->
  <property name="READABLE_LOG_PATTERN"
            value="%clr(%d{${LOG_DATEFORMAT_PATTERN:-yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss.SSSXXX}}){faint} %clr(${LOG_LEVEL_PATTERN:-%5p}){} %clr(${PID:-}){magenta} %clr(${CORRELATION_FIELDS}){faint} %clr([%15.15t]){faint} %clr(%-40.40logger{39}){cyan} %clr(:){faint} %m%n${LOG_EXCEPTION_CONVERSION_WORD:-%wEx}"/>

  <!-- ===============================================================================================
       APPENDER SELECTION BY PROFILE. Four profile treatments, in two groups.

       WHY THE STRUCTURE IS GROUPED AND NESTED RATHER THAN FLAT. An appender must be declared inside
       the profile block that references it: a declaration left at the top level is reported by the
       logging framework as declared and not referenced under every profile that does not use it,
       which is a start-up warning, and a start-up warning here is a defect. Grouping the two profiles
       that share an appender keeps each encoder written EXACTLY ONCE, so the readable layout and the
       JSON field set cannot drift apart between two copies, while the nested block inside each group
       keeps every profile's own root level and its own reasoning. Selection is by outer group,
       verbosity by inner profile.
       =============================================================================================== -->

  <!-- ===============================================================================================
       GROUP ONE, human readable: the developer facing profiles. A person reads this output, so it is
       one aligned line per event with the correlation identifiers in the line, and it is read from the
       process's own standard output, which is also what the container log carries.
       =============================================================================================== -->
  <springProfile name="local,test">

    <appender name="CONSOLE_READABLE" class="ch.qos.logback.core.ConsoleAppender">
      <encoder>
        <pattern>${READABLE_LOG_PATTERN}</pattern>
        <charset>${CONSOLE_LOG_CHARSET}</charset>
      </encoder>
    </appender>

    <!-- local: a developer's own machine, and the profile the container image activates by default.
         The compose stack provisions the dependencies while the application itself is read from its
         own output, and readiness is gated on the actuator health endpoint, so start-up has to stay
         legible: a start-up that stops short must say so in a form a person can scan. The correlation
         identifiers stay in the line, so a local flow can still be followed into the trace viewer the
         compose stack provides. -->
    <springProfile name="local">
      <root level="INFO">
        <appender-ref ref="CONSOLE_READABLE"/>
      </root>
    </springProfile>

    <!-- test: readable as well, which is exactly what the test overlay's own comment says this file
         selects for that profile. Quiet but diagnosable, and the quieting is the overlay's to do: it
         lowers the framework categories whose per statement and per step chatter, multiplied by every
         context the suite refreshes, is what makes a captured log unusable, while keeping the
         application category and the migration tool where a failure explains itself without a re-run.

         The test profile deliberately has NO trace collector, and what it switches off is EXPORT rather
         than instrumentation: application-test.yml sets management.tracing.enabled to true and
         management.otlp.tracing.export.enabled to false, so the tracer, the observation registry and
         context propagation are all wired - a test can still assert on an observation - while no
         exporter bean is created and nothing leaves the process. A line therefore carries a correlation
         field whenever a span is in scope and an empty one when none is, which is the same behaviour as
         any other profile; what differs is only that the identifiers go nowhere. That is the intended
         configuration, not a defect. -->
    <springProfile name="test">
      <root level="INFO">
        <appender-ref ref="CONSOLE_READABLE"/>
      </root>
    </springProfile>

  </springProfile>

  <!-- ===============================================================================================
       GROUP TWO, machine readable: production, and any profile this file does not name. A collector
       reads this output, so it is JSON.
       =============================================================================================== -->
  <springProfile name="!local &amp; !test">

    <!-- Machine facing output: ONE JSON OBJECT PER LINE on standard output, for a container log driver
         or a collector to ingest without a multiline rule.

         Field set, chosen to be stable and flat rather than pretty:
           @timestamp  instant, in UTC with a fixed millisecond pattern, so ordering across containers
                       in different zones is a string comparison
           level       severity
           logger      category, which is what ties an event back to the migrated program or paragraph
           thread      the executing thread, which is how concurrent batch chunks are told apart
           message     the formatted message
           stackTrace  present only when the event carries a throwable
           application the service label, matching the meter tag of the same name
           traceId     the current trace, from the diagnostic context, flattened to the top level
           spanId      the current span, likewise
           Flat rather than nested because a top level trace identifier is what a trace aware log search
           expects. The two are named individually rather than taken as the whole diagnostic context:
           the context is writable by any code on the thread and this appender's output leaves the
           process, so a key added later must be admitted here deliberately rather than published by
           default. Adding one is a single line; the rule it must satisfy is that it never reuses a field
           name listed above.

         Two encoder defaults are dropped rather than renamed: the schema version, which is a constant
         and carries no information, and the numeric duplicate of the level. Two more are switched off:
         the logging context's own properties, because the service label is already carried once as
         `application` and emitting it twice under a second name invites a query that matches half the
         events; and caller data, because capturing the call site costs a stack walk on every event
         while the category already identifies the migrated unit precisely enough to trace back to it.

         A throwable is rendered into the single stackTrace string with its newlines escaped, so the
         event remains one physical line and line oriented ingestion still works. The root cause is
         written first so it is the first thing read, and frames already printed by an enclosing cause
         are summarised rather than repeated.

         THE RENDERING IS BOUNDED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, because a throwable message is arbitrary text this
         module does not author: a driver failure carries the connection string, a constraint violation
         carries the bound values, an interpolated message carries whatever a caller submitted, and a
         rejected oversized input carries itself. Unbounded, each of those is published verbatim to a
         collector that retains it, and a caller who can provoke a failure whose message they control can
         choose how many bytes each request writes into centralised storage.

         maxDepthPerThrowable caps the frames printed for each throwable in the chain, which keeps the
         cause chain complete - every cause is still named, with its own top frames - while removing the
         repetitive tail that explains nothing. maxLength caps the whole rendered trace, so one event
         cannot grow without limit whatever the chain does. The converter marks each cut it makes, so a
         reader can tell a bound from the end of the evidence, and the frame naming the failing migrated
         unit is inside the cap because the root cause is written first.

         These bounds are a backstop for a throwable this module never sees - one raised inside the
         framework, a driver or a library. The primary control is at the boundary log sites, which record
         a sanitised, bounded failure chain and do not hand an arbitrary throwable to an appender. -->
    <appender name="CONSOLE_JSON" class="ch.qos.logback.core.ConsoleAppender">
      <encoder class="net.logstash.logback.encoder.LogstashEncoder">
        <timeZone>UTC</timeZone>
        <timestampPattern>yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss.SSS'Z'</timestampPattern>
        <!-- The diagnostic context is read, and read through an ALLOW LIST rather than wholesale.
             includeMdc keeps the provider switched on; without it the two correlation identifiers below
             would not be emitted at all. The two key names then narrow what it emits to exactly those
             two, which are the only keys this module uses. It is stated explicitly rather than left to
             the encoder default so that a future default change cannot silently drop the identifiers a
             trace aware search joins on.

             Why an allow list and not the whole context: the context is a thread local map that any code
             on the thread can write, including a library this module did not author, and THIS appender's
             output leaves the process. The values this service handles include a card primary account
             number, a card verification code, a government issued identifier and a sign on credential,
             so a key carrying one of those must be admitted here on purpose rather than published by
             default. Adding one is a single line. -->
        <includeMdc>true</includeMdc>
        <includeMdcKeyName>traceId</includeMdcKeyName>
        <includeMdcKeyName>spanId</includeMdcKeyName>
        <includeContext>false</includeContext>
        <includeCallerData>false</includeCallerData>
        <customFields>{"application":"${applicationName}"}</customFields>
        <fieldNames>
          <version>[ignore]</version>
          <levelValue>[ignore]</levelValue>
          <logger>logger</logger>
          <thread>thread</thread>
          <message>message</message>
          <stackTrace>stackTrace</stackTrace>
        </fieldNames>
        <throwableConverter class="net.logstash.logback.stacktrace.ShortenedThrowableConverter">
          <rootCauseFirst>true</rootCauseFirst>
          <omitCommonFrames>true</omitCommonFrames>
          <!-- Frames per throwable, and the whole rendered trace. Both are caps rather than targets:
               a well behaved failure is rendered in full and never reaches either. -->
          <maxDepthPerThrowable>${JSON_THROWABLE_MAX_DEPTH_PER_THROWABLE}</maxDepthPerThrowable>
          <maxLength>${JSON_THROWABLE_MAX_LENGTH}</maxLength>
        </throwableConverter>
      </encoder>
    </appender>

    <!-- prod: JSON only, on standard output, for a log driver or a collector. See
         application-prod.yml, which restates the shared baseline's four published management endpoints
         rather than narrowing to them - health, info, metrics and prometheus - withholds health
         component detail, samples traces rather than exporting all of them, requires transport
         security, and resolves every secret from the environment with no fallback.

         Publishing an endpoint there is not the same as opening it. config/SecurityConfig permits the
         health probe anonymously, because a container health check arrives without a token, and adds an
         anonymous permit for the metrics scrape path ONLY where carddemo.security.anonymous-metrics-scrape
         is set. Production leaves that key false, as the shared baseline does, so in production the
         unauthenticated surface is the health probe alone and the other three published paths -
         prometheus, metrics and info - are published but authenticated. A collector that presents a
         bearer token still scrapes.

         What this configuration guarantees about a production log line is what an encoder and an appender
         can guarantee: no statement text and no bound parameter value reaches a log, because the
         persistence provider's own statement echo is off in that overlay and the two provider categories
         at the end of this file are held at levels that emit neither. It cannot guarantee the content of
         an arbitrary application message; that is the responsibility of the code composing the message,
         and the module's diagnostic rule for it is recorded in docs/decision-log.md. -->
    <springProfile name="prod">
      <root level="WARN">
        <appender-ref ref="CONSOLE_JSON"/>
      </root>
    </springProfile>

    <!-- No profile, or any profile this file does not name. The application must NEVER be silent: an
         artefact started without a profile still logs, and it logs in the machine readable form,
         because an unnamed profile is far likelier to be a deployment than a workstation. The level is
         the shared baseline's, which is also the safe floor for a run that somehow has no overlay at
         all. -->
    <springProfile name="!prod">
      <root level="INFO">
        <appender-ref ref="CONSOLE_JSON"/>
      </root>
    </springProfile>

  </springProfile>

  <!-- ===============================================================================================
       CATEGORIES. Named here so the diagnostic surface of the migrated estate is legible in one
       place. Levels stay with the overlays, as recorded at the top of this file: a category the
       overlays name carries its agreeing baseline, and a category they do not name carries INHERITED
       so that raising the parent tree in a profile reaches it.
       =============================================================================================== -->

  <!-- The application base package, com.carddemo. Every overlay names this category, so its effective
       level is theirs. -->
  <logger name="com.carddemo" level="INFO"/>

  <!-- The batch tier: the ten migrated batch programs. Their legacy diagnostics were console displays
       inside one repeated skeleton, open, read loop, status check, close, abend, which the target
       factors into a single template step class. What that template reports is the tri state outcome
       of each operation, ok, end of file or error, and on the terminal path the RAW two byte file
       status, written into the error message BEFORE the abend is raised. That ordering and that raw
       code are reproduced deliberately: they are the diagnostic an operator of the legacy system
       recognises, and a prettified message in their place would lose the code the operator needs. -->
  <logger name="com.carddemo.batch" level="INHERITED"/>
  <logger name="com.carddemo.batch.step" level="INHERITED"/>

  <!-- The service tier: the business events the migrated programs reported. Among them the five daily
       transaction reject reason codes, 100, 101, 102, 103 and 109, each written with the record it
       rejected identified by key rather than reproduced; and the disclosure group default fallback,
       where a missing group is reported and the run continues against the default group code. A
       sensitive record-display and user-administration diagnostics use bounded outcomes, counts,
       status/reference codes and failure types rather than protected fields or provider text. Those
       source-level decisions are recorded in DL-100 and DL-152; this category declaration is not itself
       a confidentiality control. -->
  <logger name="com.carddemo.service" level="INHERITED"/>

  <!-- The request tier, standing in for the 17 screen transactions. A request scoped event carries
       the correlation identifiers and the outcome. It carries NO credential and NO full card number:
       an identifier is reported as a key, and a card is reported by the fields the screen contract
       already exposes. -->
  <logger name="com.carddemo.api" level="INHERITED"/>

  <!-- Job and step lifecycle, which is how batch progress is followed now that no job log exists.
       INFO shows a job's steps and their outcomes without the per chunk volume that would bury the
       application's own events. The shared baseline and the local overlay both name this category at
       this level; the test overlay lowers it for the suite, where per step chatter multiplied by every
       refreshed context is what makes a captured log unusable. -->
  <logger name="org.springframework.batch" level="INFO"/>

  <!-- The framework's job launcher, named here because it is the ONE category in this tree that must sit
       ABOVE its parent rather than with it. At INFO that class announces every launch as "Attempting to
       launch job with name=... and parameters=..." - the whole parameter map, VALUES INCLUDED - and the
       launch surface of this module is reached over HTTP, so those values arrive from a caller. Every
       diagnostic this module authors reports parameter KEYS and withholds their values, for the reason set
       out on com.carddemo.config.BatchConfig#batchJobBoundaryListener: a log stream leaves the process while
       the values stay readable in the framework's own execution-parameter table, which is reached through
       the database. One INFO line from inside the framework would undo that policy on every launch.
       The level itself is WARN and lives with the other levels in application.yml, as this file's own
       ownership rule requires; it is pinned in the SHARED BASELINE rather than per overlay so that raising
       the parent category in any profile cannot reach it. WARN rather than OFF because the same class
       reports genuine launch failures, and those must still be heard. Nothing is lost by the raise: the
       boundary listener still announces START OF JOB with the job name, the instance and execution
       identifiers and the sorted parameter keys, and the launch endpoint records the job and the execution
       the framework assigned. -->
  <logger name="org.springframework.batch.core.launch.support.SimpleJobOperator" level="INHERITED"/>

  <!-- Schema migration, and this one is a CROSS FILE CONTRACT rather than a preference. Local
       validation reads the application's log to confirm that every delivered migration was applied and
       that the schema reached its highest delivered version before anything else about a run is
       believed. At this level the migration tool announces each migration as it applies it, by version
       and description, which covers every delivered script - V1__create_schema, V2__create_indexes and
       V2_2__add_protected_value_invariants from the schema location, then V3__seed_reference_data and
       V4__seed_user_security from the seed location - and the two seed scripts additionally
       announce themselves by name and report what they seeded. Silencing this category would break
       that check and would also hide a start-up that stopped short of the seeds. Every overlay keeps
       it here for the same reason. -->
  <logger name="org.flywaydb" level="INFO"/>

  <!-- The migration tool's own executor, and the ONE Flyway category that must sit ABOVE its parent. At
       INFO that class announces "Database: <jdbc url> (<product version>)", "Driver: <driver and version>"
       and "Database Type: <type>" before any migration runs, so the host, the port, the database name and
       the exact server and driver versions of the estate reach the stream that leaves the process. The
       credential is already redacted from that URL, which is why the line reads as safe and is not:
       topology is what a reader needs BEFORE a credential is worth anything, and a version pair is a
       vulnerability lookup. The cross-file contract above survives the raise untouched, because every line
       it depends on is emitted by a DIFFERENT category - org.flywaydb.core.internal.command.DbMigrate -
       which stays at INFO through the parent, and because config/MigrationVersionRecordCallback now writes
       an application-owned record of each applied version and of the version reached. Level lives in
       application.yml per this file's ownership rule; declared INHERITED here so the two cannot conflict.
       See docs/decision-log.md DL-311. -->
  <logger name="org.flywaydb.core.FlywayExecutor" level="INHERITED"/>

  <!-- The queue client's two send templates. Unlike every category above, these are switched OFF rather
       than raised, and the reason is that the offending content and the useful content are the same
       statement. Both classes format the whole outbound message - payload AND headers - into their own log
       text: at TRACE and DEBUG on the way out and on success, and at ERROR together with the raw provider
       exception and the resolved endpoint when a send fails. The payload of this module's one outbound
       queue is an eighty-character job-control card, so the message IS the submitted job, and the endpoint
       is the destination nothing else here prints. No level keeps the failure and drops the card.

       Nothing is lost that this module does not say better itself. service/JobSubmissionService reports a
       refused publish naming the card's ORDINAL and never its text, and carddemo.job.submission.publish
       counts the outcome, so a failed submission is both legible and countable without a byte of the card
       leaving the process. Measured against Spring Cloud AWS 3.4.2. See docs/decision-log.md DL-311. -->
  <logger name="io.awspring.cloud.sqs.operations.AbstractMessagingTemplate" level="INHERITED"/>
  <logger name="io.awspring.cloud.sqs.operations.SqsTemplate" level="INHERITED"/>

  <!-- The trace exporter's two transports, switched off for a different reason again: the text is written
       by somebody else. On a failed export both compose their message out of the COLLECTOR's own response -
       the HTTP status line and the response body verbatim, or the transport exception's message - and the
       collector is a remote party this deployment authenticates but does not author. Anything it returns
       would be published into the machine-facing appender below, which is the output that leaves the
       process, so a compromised or merely misconfigured collector would be choosing content in this
       deployment's log stream.

       An export failure stays visible: config/ObservabilityConfig counts every failed export batch on
       carddemo.tracing.export, tagged by outcome, which is a bounded signal this module composes and which
       a dashboard can alert on. Both categories log through java.util.logging and reach this configuration
       by way of the JUL bridge the framework installs, which is why naming them here works at all.
       Measured against OpenTelemetry 1.56.0, and re-confirmed at that release rather than carried
       forward: the two categories below are internal names, so DL-358 verified both still exist under
       exactly these names before the version moved. See docs/decision-log.md DL-311 and DL-358. -->
  <logger name="io.opentelemetry.exporter.internal.http.HttpExporter" level="INHERITED"/>
  <logger name="io.opentelemetry.exporter.internal.grpc.GrpcExporter" level="INHERITED"/>

  <!-- The two persistence provider categories that would otherwise put data into the log. The first
       writes the statement text, the second writes the bound parameter values, and both write only at
       DEBUG, so declaring them at INFO means neither is emitted under any profile unless a profile
       asks for it by name.

       The parameter category is the sensitive one: a bound parameter can be a card primary account
       number or a national identifier, so it must never be raised in production, and nothing raises
       it here. Where a developer does want to see statements, the local overlay turns on the
       provider's OWN statement echo through its own switch rather than through this category, which
       keeps the two decisions separate.

       THIS IS ONE HALF OF ONE RULE. The other half is in docker-compose.yml, where the database's
       slow-statement diagnostic is given log_parameter_max_length=0 so the server cannot print the
       values a statement was bound with either - its default is to print them in full. Closing one
       channel and leaving the other open would read as though both were closed. Both halves are
       asserted: this one by ObservabilityConfigTest, the other by ContainerHardeningContractTest. -->
  <logger name="org.hibernate.SQL" level="INFO"/>
  <logger name="org.hibernate.orm.jdbc.bind" level="INFO"/>

  <!-- The servlet container's cookie parser, and this one is CONFIDENTIALITY rather than legibility.
       When a request arrives with a Cookie header the parser cannot read, it reports the refusal by
       printing the header it received IN FULL, at INFO for the first occurrence and at DEBUG for every
       occurrence after that. A client that presents its bearer credential in a Cookie header instead of
       an Authorization header, which this application correctly declines to authenticate, therefore had
       its entire still valid token written into the log in clear text, where a token read back out of it
       authenticated. The container's categories reach this logging system because the framework bridges
       the container's own logging to it, so a category declared here governs them.

       Declared at WARN rather than switched off, and that is the point of the level: the record exists
       only at INFO and at DEBUG, so WARN closes BOTH forms of it while leaving a genuinely more serious
       report from the same class audible. It is declared here in the shared block rather than per
       profile, so raising a profile's root level cannot reach it: the production profile's root already
       sat above INFO and so never emitted the record, while every other profile did.

       Narrow on purpose. Silencing the container's parsing categories wholesale would also hide the
       protocol diagnostics that explain a malformed request, which is exactly the evidence a local gate
       run depends on. One emitter was found printing a credential, so one emitter is named. Recorded as
       decision DL-266 and asserted by ObservabilityConfigTest. -->
  <logger name="org.apache.tomcat.util.http.parser.Cookie" level="WARN"/>

</configuration>
